1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:04,532
A running fan sounds reassuring. In this food production zone,

2
00:00:04,532 --> 00:00:06,741
the airflow tells a different story.

3
00:00:07,061 --> 00:00:09,088
OpenSoom follows one ventilation decision,

4
00:00:09,088 --> 00:00:13,953
from the first observation through to the result a manager can verify.

5
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:18,366
A Korean study assessed 301 schools

6
00:00:18,366 --> 00:00:21,568
after ventilation upgrades in their kitchens.

7
00:00:21,888 --> 00:00:25,167
In that group, 17.9% still received

8
00:00:25,167 --> 00:00:28,445
an inadequate rating. That finding raises a practical question.

9
00:00:28,765 --> 00:00:32,517
Once equipment is operating, how do you check the result in each zone?

10
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:36,116
This is the actual openSoom dashboard,

11
00:00:36,116 --> 00:00:41,309
connected to a running oneM2M service. Its sensors and actuators are simulated.

12
00:00:41,629 --> 00:00:46,129
The cooking line, wash station and packing area each have their own profile.

13
00:00:46,129 --> 00:00:49,936
Selecting the cooking line brings up the measurements behind its alert.

14
00:00:50,256 --> 00:00:53,709
The fan reports that it is running. Airflow is three hundred

15
00:00:53,709 --> 00:00:57,476
and sixty cubic metres per hour. This zone calls for nine hundred.

16
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:02,722
From the zone inspection, the operator requests a fully open damper.

17
00:01:02,722 --> 00:01:06,924
The service checks the facility, permission and freshness of the observation.

18
00:01:07,244 --> 00:01:09,399
The device acknowledges the command.

19
00:01:09,399 --> 00:01:13,278
OpenSoom waits for two fresh observations after that acknowledgement.

20
00:01:13,598 --> 00:01:16,045
On this run the airflow reaches one thousand

21
00:01:16,045 --> 00:01:18,492
and thirty five, and the incident can close.

22
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:23,154
A second supplier should be able to join without rewriting the dashboard.

23
00:01:23,154 --> 00:01:25,879
Here is a recorded example of what oneM2M contributes.

24
00:01:26,199 --> 00:01:29,618
A device creates an observation over MQTT with TLS.

25
00:01:29,618 --> 00:01:33,037
A management application retrieves the same resource over HTTPS.

26
00:01:33,357 --> 00:01:35,950
Both responses carry the same resource identifier.

27
00:01:35,950 --> 00:01:38,542
The standard platform connects the two protocols.

28
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:43,528
At the second facility, the same dashboard opens a learning studio,

29
00:01:43,528 --> 00:01:46,079
a meeting room and a shared lounge.

30
00:01:46,399 --> 00:01:49,409
This supplier reports airflow in litres per second.

31
00:01:49,409 --> 00:01:55,052
The adapter converts it into cubic metres per hour before the operating tools read it.

32
00:01:55,372 --> 00:01:59,848
The studio is at two hundred against its own five hundred target.

33
00:01:59,848 --> 00:02:02,459
The operator requests the same damper action.

34
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:07,837
In this run the damper acknowledges the request and reaches one hundred percent.

35
00:02:07,837 --> 00:02:10,493
The next observations still show only two hundred.

36
00:02:10,813 --> 00:02:14,529
The service keeps the incident open. A maintenance technician can

37
00:02:14,529 --> 00:02:18,616
review the command, acknowledgement and later readings together.

38
00:02:18,936 --> 00:02:21,514
Both runs followed the same procedure.

39
00:02:21,514 --> 00:02:24,521
Each produced a different, traceable decision.

40
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:28,534
A separate test ran two applications.

41
00:02:28,534 --> 00:02:31,548
Operations and maintenance ran as independent processes.

42
00:02:31,868 --> 00:02:37,532
Each has its own oneM2M application identity and subscription. A device publishes one abnormal observation.

43
00:02:37,532 --> 00:02:41,308
Both applications receive a notification and retrieve the underlying resource.

44
00:02:41,628 --> 00:02:44,307
Operations requests a permitted action for the zone.

45
00:02:44,307 --> 00:02:48,177
Maintenance opens an inspection order for the same event.

46
00:02:48,497 --> 00:02:51,366
The service platform handles delivery and access control.

47
00:02:51,366 --> 00:02:53,518
Each application owns a decision.

48
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:57,511
These records come from that separate software run.

49
00:02:57,511 --> 00:03:01,249
The event identifier connects the original observation to both outputs.

50
00:03:01,569 --> 00:03:04,777
The operations report records what happened to the command.

51
00:03:04,777 --> 00:03:09,054
The maintenance work order gives inspection staff a traceable reason to investigate.

52
00:03:09,374 --> 00:03:13,890
A recorded airflow recovery cannot certify that a technician inspected the equipment.

53
00:03:13,890 --> 00:03:16,524
The work order remains a separate responsibility.

54
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:20,797
A different role should not silently inherit equipment control.

55
00:03:20,797 --> 00:03:24,438
In the separate test, maintenance tries to issue a command.

56
00:03:24,758 --> 00:03:26,856
The oneM2M platform denies the write.

57
00:03:26,856 --> 00:03:30,353
Separate role tests also block an observer reading another facility.

58
00:03:30,673 --> 00:03:33,423
The demo runs locally.

59
00:03:33,423 --> 00:03:36,517
A working facility also needs authentication and commissioning.

60
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:39,952
A manager can also use an agent.

61
00:03:39,952 --> 00:03:44,469
The Codex plugin and a standalone model runner share the same operating tools.

62
00:03:44,789 --> 00:03:48,553
Here a hosted model discovered resources, read a group,

63
00:03:48,553 --> 00:03:52,735
examined evidence, requested an action and checked the result twice.

64
00:03:53,055 --> 00:03:57,449
The first check was still waiting. The second found the required observations.

65
00:03:57,449 --> 00:04:01,477
The server retained the run independently of the model&#x27;s written answer.

66
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:06,763
In a separate recorded experiment, a central service connected two local facilities.

67
00:04:06,763 --> 00:04:10,299
We interrupted the central service for just over fourteen seconds.

68
00:04:10,619 --> 00:04:14,760
The food facility continued its local command, acknowledgement and recovery check.

69
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:18,525
When the connection returned, the service rejected an expired request.

70
00:04:18,845 --> 00:04:22,520
The local facility kept the same serving platform.

71
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:27,134
The repository includes both facility profiles, the two supplier adapters,

72
00:04:27,134 --> 00:04:30,748
and a deterministic demonstration that runs without an AI account.

73
00:04:31,068 --> 00:04:33,546
A maintainer installs and checks the service.

74
00:04:33,546 --> 00:04:37,677
A manager can inspect the facility from the dashboard or Codex plugin.

75
00:04:37,997 --> 00:04:41,213
To adapt another site, configure its zones and targets,

76
00:04:41,213 --> 00:04:45,502
map the supplier units, and validate observations before enabling any physical control.

77
00:04:46,850 --> 00:04:49,623
OpenSoom gives facilities a shared way to

78
00:04:49,623 --> 00:04:52,395
ask what changed after ventilation equipment acted.

79
00:04:52,715 --> 00:04:55,617
Explore the code, replay the scenarios and

80
00:04:55,617 --> 00:04:58,934
inspect the underlying evidence in the openSoom repository.
